i have a very clear memory of playing  time splitters future perfect for hours  and hours over one rainy easter weekend  back in 2005. i was 13 i had a generous  supply of chocolate eggs a tiny crt tv  and most importantly no responsibilities  whatsoever it's one of those blissful  memories that if you grew up playing  games will be permanently etched in your  mind  life was good  [Music]  now look at me  when i was growing up i played time  splits 2 and future perfect on an almost  constant basis with my mates enjoying  its mad split screen multiplayer and  trying to get every challenge mode medal  but it's been 16  long years since the last time splitters  game and shooting games in particular  have changed pretty dramatically since  then so how well does time splitters  hold up in 2021  nowadays it's surprisingly difficult to  actually find a way to play them if you  don't have access to the original  consoles the only time splitters game to  get any kind of a modern remaster is  time splitters 2 whose story mode can be  found hidden away on an arcade cabinet  in home front the revolution they don't  even appear on xbox's list of backwards  compatible games there's also the fan  produced time splitters rewind project  but that is still some way off from  completion back in may deep silver  announced the reformation of free  radical design creators of the original  time splitters trilogy to be led by  original co-founders steve ellis and  david doke who have just a little bit of  experience creating great shooter games  and haze not only that but free radicals  first task is a new entry to the beloved  time splitters franchise brilliant i  thought with e3 around the corner surely  a remastered trilogy is on the cards but  sadly e3 came and went with neria monkey  in sight so  i just got to sit here twiddling my  thumbs right i mean what am i gonna do  borrow my partner's dad's old ps2 which  only has one half working third party  controller buy an hdmi adapter so it  works with the only tvs that i've got  and try and scrounge up copies of the  games from whatever secondhand shops  have them locally  yeah  before i relive my childhood here's a  little history lesson to get everyone up  to speed time splitters was released  exclusively for playstation 2 way back  in the year 2000 and if that makes you  feel odd don't worry because the game  feels even older unlike later entries  time splitter's story mode is a bare  bones trudge through boilerplate levels  to retrieve a key followed by a mad dash  back the way you came as for multiplayer  it's the usual selection of modes and  maps and shares the single player's  chaotic n64 shooter feel and rounding  out the feature set is a map maker and a  challenge mode that only unlocks upon  completing the story mode i never had a  ps2 growing up and the world isn't  exactly conducive to split screen gaming  right now so it's hard to really get a  sense for how it would have been at  launch according to the box revered  gaming magazine edge claimed it was the  game worth buying a playstation 2 for so  it must have been pretty good but having  grown up playing the sequels to me it  all feels a little bit first draft it  does at least set the tone for the  series i mean where else can you shoot  gingerbread men ducks and elvis  impersonators in the same game  but even if you loved the first game i  think it's fair to say that time splits  2 is where free radical really hit their  stride  time splitters 2 featured a proper story  mode across a variety of sprawling  levels with extra objectives or  complications appearing depending on  difficulty level much like golden eye  and perfect dark the location and time  hopping story took in settings as varied  as notre dame siberia alien planets and  a rainy neo tokyo all the way in the  distant future of uh  2019 along the way you'll face off  against soldiers zombies robots the  titular time splitters themselves and  perhaps most nefarious of all  this spiral staircase that for some  reason gave me such intense motion  sickness that i had to go for a lie down  stairs aside it's astonishing how well  it holds up in 2021 and how much variety  is crammed into every single level the  opening mission alone sees you  infiltrate a compound sneak through the  interior investigate a mysterious  digging site uncover cells full of  zombies and mutants fight your way back  out as special forces arrive and destroy  a helicopter from some kind of bridge i  mean damn it is an absolute treat  featuring the kind of creative  open-ended design that is sorely missing  from so many single-player campaigns in  modern first-person shooters  and then there's the mighty player  oh man the multiplayer  there's two variants of arcade mode with  the arcade league challenging you to  pre-set scenarios each with their own  absurd lawlets as if you need them and  then the endless toy box fun of arcade  custom it is chaotic messy uncomplicated  fun with a wealth of customizable  options characters maps weapons and if  all that isn't enough the map maker  returns to let you create your own  levels once you're bored of the usual  lot it's a feature set that puts modern  games to shame topped off with the  improved challenge mode that sees you  beheading undead smashing windows with  bricks or sneaking past cameras to earn  medals expect this to return with a  vengeance and online leaderboards in any  future time splitters game if you're  anything like me it's no surprise that  time splitters 2 became a staple of your  multiplayer gaming back in the early  noughties  time splitter's future perfect the third  game in the series shifts the balance of  its single-player campaign to lose some  of the sandbox feel in favor of some  halo-esque bombast as well as adding a  more developed but incredibly cheesy  story into the mix it certainly makes  more of its larger-than-life characters  though whether that's a good thing is  debatable some of the character designs  have aged poorly and some are just plain  annoying are you sure bean this whole  area is about to be bombed to kingdom  come i mean what is that accent supposed  to be  with those caveats aside it's still an  enjoyable romp if not quite as free-form  as its predecessor challenge mode  returns complete with remote controlled  cat racing obviously and the multiplayer  offers more characters to choose from a  few bigger maps and expanded map maker  is basically everything you'd expect  from a sequel to time splitters 2. much  the same but much more of it  playing these games again in 2021 it's  interesting to think where a remaster or  a sequel would sit in the pantheon of  modern day first-person shooters i mean  in retrospect i'm kind of glad we  skipped a few generations we probably  missed out on a misjudged grim reboot in  the 360 generation where everything's  gray and brown and we've probably  avoided a shoehorned time splitters  battle royale mode too  [Music]  actually that does sound pretty good  i mean i'd be rubbish at it the trilogy  also feels like a kind of window into a  lost era of gaming there are 150  characters in future perfect multiplayer  if it was released now how many do you  think would be locked behind dlc season  passes or pre-order bonuses there's no  xp grind no time limited events seasonal  tie-ins or pay-to-win weapons excuse the  nostalgia but they're like a peek into  the innocent halcyon days of console  gaming that are probably never coming  back the other side of that coin is that  it come from a kind of bygone era of  console gaming before online multiplayer  really took hold and to me that's kind  of a crucial part of their charm  in my head the time splitters games are  always going to be split screen couch  co-op party games they're games that  deserve to be played with your mates in  easy reach for high fiving  or punching if the new time splitters  game can capture the tone the character  and the chaos of the originals it will  feel like a total outlier in modern  shooters too many games are drab and  serious too many multiplayer games ask  you to dedicate an inordinate amount of  time to them if you want to stay  competitive if a time splitter's sequel  is to stay true to the originals it  needs to carry on in that spirit and  just focus on giving players a good time  not enough games lean into uncomplicated  fun or the chance to be downright silly  not enough games capture that feeling  when you're 13 you've got tons of  chocolate and not a care in the world  i'm sure i'm not the only one looking  forward to the next time splits game so  let me know in the comments down below  what you would like to see in the  meantime if you've enjoyed the video  drop us a like and if you want to see  more content like this hit subscribe  thanks very much for watching i'll see  you next time  [Music]  so  [Music]  you
 

